Let $G$ be a finite group with properties $2$ and that has no 2-cycle as an extra element, and let $Z$ be the complex Z-module representing a one-dimensional complex representation of $G$. When the conditions $H^{3}_{
eq 0}(Z[[G]])_{\hG}$ and $H^{3}_{
eq 0}(Z[[G]]])_{\hG}$ are met, we demonstrate that the homotopy orbit spectrum with the projection map $Z 	o Z/G$ being homotopy equivalent to the spectrum $Z$. Conversely, we establish that if the homotopy orbit spectrum with the projection map $Z 	o Z/G$ is homotopy equivalent to the spectrum $Z$ then the conditions $H^{3}_{
eq 0}(Z[[G]])_{\hG}$ and $H^{3}_{
eq 0}(Z[[G]]])_{\hG}$ are satisfied.

We investigate the asymptotic growth of index degeneracies among physically distinct BPS states, forming spectracorrespondences under S-duality. These states reside within the set of points on the Hilbert scheme of a general K smooth curves of length 2, denoted as Hilb$_{\mathop{\mathrm{Hilb}}
olimits_{2}^{(K)}}$, where $\mathop{\mathrm{Hilb}}
olimits_{2}^{(K)}$ is the moduli space of stable rank 2 vectors on a general curve, with the element having a length of 2. Our findings reveal an exponential increase in index degeneracies. Additionally, we establish an analogy between these degeneracies and the Chevalley's theorem for symplectic algebraic geometry, discovering an affine patch structure for the effective divisor $\mathop{\mathrm{Hilb}}
olimits_{2}^{(K)}$ in the Picard group of $\mathop{\mathrm{Hilb}}
olimits_{2}^{(K)}$.
